WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:06.960
I have a friend and mentor who is known as&nbsp;
the Master of Segways. And so in this video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:13.920
I'm excited to share how he would change the&nbsp;
topic of conversation. Because oftentimes you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:20.000
might be in a conversation and it's not... You're&nbsp;
not really feeling it. And you want to shift&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:26.880
topics. And it could be at a holiday with your&nbsp;
family talking about politics which you really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:32.000
would like to avoid. Or it could just be at work&nbsp;
and you just need to steer a discussion so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.600
it's a more productive meeting. In this video,&nbsp;
I'm going to share a funny way from my mentor to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.360
change the topic of conversation. I'm going&nbsp;
to share a clever way to change the topic of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:57.840
conversation. And I'm going to share an honest way&nbsp;
to change the topic of conversation. Here we go.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:02.720
Alright, if this happens to be the first time&nbsp;
that we're meeting in cyberspace, I'm Chad and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:07.120
my partner and I wrote a book called Ask Powerful&nbsp;
Questions Create Conversations That Matter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:14.000
And so, while I have... I feel like I've learned&nbsp;
about 2% of what it takes to make meaningful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:20.320
conversations happen to navigate difficult topics.&nbsp;
And while I have 98% to learn, I've got some stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:24.400
that I think would be really useful for you and&nbsp;
which is why exactly why I've recorded this video.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:30.080
And so funny way, funny way to change the&nbsp;
topic of conversation came from a good friend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:35.520
and mentor Brenton who I worked with at an&nbsp;
organization called World in Conversation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:42.800
And in the group, what he would do as a way&nbsp;
of segwaying conversation, he had a formula.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:50.320
And it was you just insert speaking of ___. And&nbsp;
you insert whatever the person was speaking about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:59.280
Let's talk about ___. Whatever he wanted&nbsp;
to talk about. So, speaking of politics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:03.840
who's hungry? Right? And so,&nbsp;
I ended up being though... And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:08.480
there's no way that I can fully capture&nbsp;
the magic that he did it with. It was just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:13.120
every single segway that he had was just&nbsp;
created a little bit of joy. But somehow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:21.360
it actually did work. So, even though it&nbsp;
was obviously abrupt and a bit crazy even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:26.400
in the way that he segued by saying, "Speaking&nbsp;
of..." whatever we're talking about, "Let's talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:32.480
about ___." Insert something completely irrelevant&nbsp;
that he's changing the topic to. For some reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:38.160
it worked. And so, I'd invite you to actually try&nbsp;
out that formula. Speaking of ____, let's talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:44.160
about ___. Or insert whatever it is that you want&nbsp;
to talk about at the end. So, speaking of is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:50.320
key phrase to tuck into your back pocket. Because&nbsp;
to change the topic completely out of the blue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:54.800
feels really jarring. But when you reference&nbsp;
what's currently being talked about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.800 --> 00:03:01.200
and then offer the new topic, it doesn't feel like&nbsp;
such a far leap or jump. So, that's the funny way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:07.360
The clever way to change the topic of&nbsp;
conversation is to follow your curiosity. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:13.520
conversation is exponential. And we forget that&nbsp;
sometimes. We think because we're talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:18.560
one particular sort of umbrella topic that that's&nbsp;
the only... We're kind of stuck in that lane.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:26.960
And yet, in conversation, no matter what anybody's&nbsp;
saying about whatever topic, you are learning tons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:31.600
of different information, right? So, if you ask&nbsp;
me "Hey, Chad. What's the story of the ring on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:39.040
your finger?" I might tell you about how I met&nbsp;
Kate and that entire story and development. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:43.360
in me telling you that, you would obviously&nbsp;
learn the facts that I shared with you. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:48.880
you'd also learn something about how I feel about&nbsp;
Kate based on the energy that I'm talking with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:54.240
You would learn a little bit about me and my&nbsp;
way of thinking from this. Even though I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:59.040
not specifically talking about that. And so, the&nbsp;
clever way to change the topic of conversation is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:06.640
to listen for what you're naturally curious about&nbsp;
and ask a question rooted in that curiosity to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:12.480
guide the conversation that way. Because there's&nbsp;
plenty of data in the room to take a conversation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:18.320
in any direction. And so, when I say conversations&nbsp;
exponential, let's say you learned... Somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:24.560
said was talking for about a minute. Let's say&nbsp;
you learned 5 things. Each of those five things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:29.760
could be a separate question. It could be turned&nbsp;
into a separate question and a separate path of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:34.640
conversation. And so, if you want to change the&nbsp;
topic of conversation, if 3 of the things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:40.640
you learned were about that topic, don't ask about&nbsp;
those. Ask about the ones that were brought up in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:46.320
the conversation but don't necessarily really&nbsp;
relate. Now, this is a bit of a passive way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:50.480
change the topic of conversation. So, you may&nbsp;
be met with some resistance from people saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:57.120
"What do you mean like..." Like, "That's not what&nbsp;
we're talking about." And they might want to pull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:03.120
you back into that current topic. And if that's&nbsp;
the case, if that happens, for me, the third&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:10.240
and probably the best way to change the topic of&nbsp;
conversation is the honest way. And the honest way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:16.160
to change the topic of conversation is to point&nbsp;
out the elephant in the room and acknowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:22.000
that whatever you're talking about right now is&nbsp;
not working or is not productive or is not what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:28.160
you'd like to talk about. Or just state what it&nbsp;
is or why it is you'd like to change the topic of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:37.040
conversation. And then do it. So, state what you'd&nbsp;
like... State why you would like to shift the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:44.800
conversation by noticing the dynamic that's&nbsp;
currently present in the room. And then say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:50.240
"Let's talk about ___." Now, depending on the&nbsp;
group, depending on the context, you may want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:57.040
seek permission so to say, "Hey, I feel like we've&nbsp;
are talking about this a little bit in circles. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:06.240
only have 30 minutes with each other. Can we table&nbsp;
this right now and talk about ___ instead? Can we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:13.520
table the discussion about the game last night and&nbsp;
talk about what we have to get done so that we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:17.760
end our meeting on time?" Do you see how in that&nbsp;
I point out the elephant in the room? I took a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:23.600
break from the content of the conversation. And&nbsp;
I pointed out and accessed the process of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:31.040
conversation. And then I invited the group or&nbsp;
the person to come with me on that journey. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:37.600
in order to really and deeply change the topic&nbsp;
of conversation, you've got to point out what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:44.560
currently happening and why that might not be&nbsp;
working for you. Let me give you one add-on tip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:50.720
to the honest way to change a conversation.&nbsp;
And that is... Sometimes if you for whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:54.960
reason you don't feel comfortable pointing out the&nbsp;
dynamic, pointing out the elephant in the room or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.960 --> 00:07:00.160
saying you don't want to talk about whatever is&nbsp;
currently being discussed, you can state your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:06.000
intention really clearly for why and what you&nbsp;
want to change the topic to. So, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:10.960
let's say we were talking about lions but we&nbsp;
wanted to talk... I wanted to talk about zebras.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:17.200
So, I'm using a generic example so that you&nbsp;
can insert your own dilemma here. So, if you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:22.160
watching this video because you really want to&nbsp;
figure out how to change the topic from politics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:28.640
to what people are going to do next week, right?&nbsp;
You can fill in that blank. So, if we're talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:34.160
about lions and I want to talk about zebras, I&nbsp;
could say, "Hey, I think we've talked about lions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:41.440
enough. I'm kind of done. Can we move on and chat&nbsp;
about zebras?" Or you could leave the lions out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:44.960
of it and you could just say... If you're in the&nbsp;
middle of talking about lions, you could just say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:52.160
"Hey, I would love right now to shift the&nbsp;
conversation a little bit and talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:57.440
zebras instead. Is everybody okay with that?"&nbsp;
Stating that intention might be enough. Now, if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:02.400
you've got a particularly rebellious or resistant&nbsp;
group or a group that really likes lions and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:08.240
don't want to let go of the lions, what you might&nbsp;
do is state your intention to talk about zebras&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:15.920
really clearly framed a as a value to the group.&nbsp;
So, to say something like, "Hey, we've got limited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:21.520
amount of time and I would love to connect more&nbsp;
with all of you so can we talk about zebras&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:25.920
instead? Can we make that shift?" So, you see&nbsp;
you're kind of framing the benefit of saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:29.520
"Hey, I think this would be actually more&nbsp;
useful more productive to chat about this."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:35.280
Now, in any of these strategies that I shared,&nbsp;
is it possible that somebody just says, "No, I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:39.440
talking about lions. I'm going to continue to talk&nbsp;
about lions. You can't do anything about it. I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:46.880
going to talk about lions." Yes, that is possible&nbsp;
and yet unlikely. Because we have a desire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:54.320
to fit in and belong. And so, if there's enough of&nbsp;
